// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// EVERY PROGRAM IN THIS FILE IS SUPPOSED TO BE REJECTED.
//
// Do not fix them. The file is the corpus's only **safety** probe: twenty
// other programs ask whether the language can express something, and this one
// asks whether it refuses what it says it refuses.
//
// Each case carries four things the design documents do not, because they are
// about the *wrong* program rather than the right one:
//
//   WANTED   — what the author was trying to do. A rejection that cannot name
//              the intent is usually a bad API rather than a bad program.
//   WHY      — why the refusal is worth its cost, in this design's terms.
//   SAYS     — what the compiler should print. `diagnostics.md`: the
//              diagnostic *is* the teaching.
//   INSTEAD  — the spelling that works.
//
// The rule citation stays one line, because the documents own that. These
// four are what this file is for.
//
// **And writing SAYS turned out to be the test.** A rule whose message cannot
// be written is a rule that is not finished — it is naming a mechanism, a
// duration or a property that nobody has decided. That is a sharper criterion
// than rereading the rule and wondering, and it is what sorts the verdicts:
//
//   REFUSED     — the rule exists and the message is writable.
//   UNFINISHED  — the rule exists and **the message cannot be written**.
//   UNCAUGHT    — no rule.
//
// Sixteen cases: seven REFUSED, five UNFINISHED, four UNCAUGHT.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

import { Confined, Synchronized, Shared } from elp.concurrency
import { Error } from elp.types

export object Doc {
	var lines [string],
}

export object Node {
	var value int,
	indirect var next Optional<Node>,
}

// ── 1. REFUSED — a cell crossing a thread ────────────────────────────────────
// RULE     `aliasing.md`: `Confined<T>` is never `Sendable`, under any `T`.
// WANTED   To read the document from a worker without copying it.
// WHY      `Confined<T>` is `Rc<RefCell<T>>`. Two threads refcounting one
//          allocation is a data race before anything touches the contents, so
//          the refusal is about the handle rather than about `Doc`.
// SAYS     "`Confined<Doc>` cannot cross a thread boundary: it is never
//          `Sendable`. Use `Synchronized<Doc>` if the worker must write, or
//          `Shared<Doc>` if the document is immutable."
// INSTEAD  `Shared<Doc>` — this body only reads.
fn case1(doc Confined<Doc>) {
	background(fn {
		doc.get().lines.length()
	})
}

// ── 2. REFUSED — two mutable paths to one place ──────────────────────────────
// RULE     `memory-model.md` Finding 4's third amendment, rule 5.
// WANTED   A swap, which is the honest answer: swapping is the operation the
//          exclusivity rule is worst at and `examples/Ledger` FINDING 1 is the
//          same shape one level up.
// WHY      Two live mutable paths to one storage is the aliasing bug the whole
//          model exists to prevent, and it cannot be made safe by being
//          obviously silly — the two arguments are usually `xs[i]` and `xs[j]`.
// SAYS     "`name` is already borrowed mutably by argument 1. Two `&var`
//          arguments to one call may not name the same storage."
// INSTEAD  Nothing, today. A swap has no spelling, and that is `tradeoffs.md`'s
//          entry with no substitute.
fn swapBadly(a &var string, b &var string) {}

fn case2() {
	var name = "x"
	swapBadly(&var name, &var name)
}

// ── 3. REFUSED — a field holding a borrow ────────────────────────────────────
// RULE     `aliasing.md`, the field-wise precisions.
// WANTED   To keep a reference to something owned elsewhere, which is what
//          every programmer coming from a GC'd language reaches for first.
// WHY      A borrow is bounded by the call that produced it; a field is
//          bounded by nothing. And `&T` is unconditionally `Copy`, so a `&T`
//          field is a duplication route that field-wise approves without
//          looking at `T` at all.
// SAYS     "A field may not hold a borrow. `&string` is bounded by a call and
//          a field outlives one. Hold a `string`, or a `Shared<string>` if the
//          value is shared."
// INSTEAD  `const borrowed Shared<string>`.
export object Holder {
	const borrowed &string,
}

// ── 4. REFUSED — writing through an indirect field ───────────────────────────
// RULE     `memory-model.md`, `indirect` rule 4. `write_through_indirect`.
// WANTED   To edit a linked list in place, which is what a linked list is for
//          in every other language.
// WHY      The box is immutable *so that the containing type stays `Copy`*. A
//          mutable box would be `Confined`-shaped and would merge this gap
//          with the write-surface problem. The refusal is the price of `Node`
//          being a value.
// SAYS     "`next` is an `indirect` field, so its contents may not be written.
//          Replace the field instead: `node.next = .SOME(updated)`."
// INSTEAD  Rebuild the path. That is what a persistent structure does anyway,
//          and `examples/Editor`'s undo needs it.
fn case4(node &var Node) {
	node.next.value = 5
}

// ── 5. REFUSED — a `pure fn` reading a mutable cell ──────────────────────────
// RULE     `functions.md`, the closed set.
// WANTED   A helper that is "obviously a calculation" and happens to look at
//          some shared state.
// WHY      Two calls with the same arguments can return two answers, which is
//          the one property `pure` sells. The cell is the input the signature
//          does not mention.
// SAYS     "`case5` is declared `pure` and reads a `Confined<int>`. Reading a
//          mutable cell is not pure: pass the value in instead."
// INSTEAD  Sample it at the caller — which is exactly what
//          `examples/Playhead`'s `estimate` does, for an unrelated reason, and
//          is evidence the rule matches practice.
pure fn case5(counter &Confined<int>) -> int {
	return counter.get()
}

// ── 6. REFUSED — a `pure fn` declaring `&var` ────────────────────────────────
// RULE     `memory-model.md` Finding 5, now a rule rather than a consequence.
// WANTED   An accumulator, which is the ordinary way to write a recursive
//          collector — `examples/Json`'s writer does exactly this and is not
//          `pure`.
// WHY      `pure` promises the caller's values are unchanged. A `&var`
//          parameter is a promise they are not.
// SAYS     "A `pure fn` may not declare a `&var` parameter. Drop `pure`, or
//          return the value instead of accumulating into one."
// INSTEAD  Drop `pure`. An accumulator is not impure in any interesting sense,
//          and this is the one REFUSED case where the rule is arguably
//          stricter than it needs to be.
pure fn case6(out &var [string]) {
	out.append("no")
}

// ── 7. REFUSED — `const` where mutation is required ──────────────────────────
// RULE     Finding 4's surviving half: the referent's `const`ness is checked
//          against the declared `&var`.
// WANTED   To pass something to a function without noticing the signature
//          mutates it, which is the mistake the declaration-site `&var` exists
//          to surface.
// WHY      This is the case that justifies writing `&var` at the call site at
//          all: without the marker the error points at a signature in another
//          file, and with it the error points at the line you wrote.
// SAYS     "`fixed` is `const` and `swapBadly` writes through this argument.
//          Declare it `var` if it needs to change."
// INSTEAD  `var fixed = "x"`.
fn case7() {
	const fixed = "x"
	swapBadly(&var fixed, &var fixed)
}

// ── 8. UNFINISHED — mutating a collection while iterating it ─────────────────
// RULE     `control-flow.md` claims this "needs no rule of its own".
// WANTED   To grow a list while walking it, which is a real algorithm — a
//          worklist, a flood fill, a transitive closure.
// WHY      The claim is that `iterator()` takes `&self` and `append` takes
//          `&var self`, so the two overlap.
// SAYS     — **cannot be written.** The message would have to say *how long*
//          the iterator's borrow lasts, and no document says. If it ends at
//          each `next()`, this is legal and the claim is false. If it spans
//          the loop, the message is "`xs` is borrowed by the loop", and
//          nothing states that it does.
// INSTEAD  Collect into a second list and append after the loop — which is
//          what every language that refuses this tells you, and it is the
//          right answer; it is the *rule* that is missing, not the ergonomics.
fn case8(xs &var [string]) {
	for x in xs {
		xs.append(x)
	}
}

// ── 9. UNFINISHED — assigning through an index ───────────────────────────────
// RULE     `operators.md`: `Index` only reads.
// WANTED   The most ordinary statement in programming.
// WHY      "Collections hold values, not places" exists so an API that forgets
//          a write-back is a visible no-op rather than an invisible aliasing
//          bug. Assignment through an index is the one case where the write-
//          back is not forgettable, which is why this refusal is the least
//          comfortable in the file.
// SAYS     — **cannot be written**, because nothing says what this *is*. A
//          parse error, an unknown-method error on `Index`, or an unimplemented
//          feature are three different messages and the record picks none.
// INSTEAD  `xs.update(0, fn (&var s) { s = "no" })`, two closures deep for an
//          assignment. `examples/Editor` FINDING 6 is the corpus's first
//          evidence *for* an assignable index rather than against one.
fn case9(xs &var [string]) {
	xs[0] = "no"
}

// ── 10. UNFINISHED — allocating in a real-time body ──────────────────────────
// RULE     `functions.md`: `realtime` forbids allocation.
// WANTED   A log line. This is how every audio glitch bug in the world starts.
// WHY      An allocation takes a lock in the allocator, and a lock on a thread
//          with a 23ms deadline is a click in the output. The rule is the
//          whole reason `realtime` is a body property.
// SAYS     — **cannot be written**, and this is the clearest case of the
//          pattern. The intended message is in `functions.md` already —
//          *"`\"{frame}\"` allocates; a real-time body may not allocate"* —
//          and it depends on stdlib operations carrying an allocates-or-not
//          property, which that file costed and nobody built. So the rule
//          cannot currently fire on anything at all.
// INSTEAD  Publish a counter and format it on the reader's thread, which is
//          what `examples/Playhead` does.
@abi(.C, realtime)
realtime fn case10(frames int) -> int {
	const label = "frames: {frames}"

	return label.length()
}

// ── 11. UNFINISHED — a transform reaching a second lock ──────────────────────
// RULE     `aliasing.md`: a `Synchronized` transform must not `background`,
//          receive from a channel, or reach into another `Synchronized`.
// WANTED   To combine two counters, which is the most natural thing to do with
//          two counters.
// WHY      Lock ordering. Two transforms taking the same two locks in opposite
//          orders deadlock, and `operators.md` makes yield points invisible so
//          there is nothing in the source to see it coming.
// SAYS     — **cannot be written.** The rule is "a restriction on a body by
//          position" and `aliasing.md` records that it has **no mechanism**:
//          `pure` was the candidate and `examples/Interpreter` FINDING 8 ruled
//          it out. A message needs something to point at, and the body
//          property that would catch this is the queued third one.
// INSTEAD  Read `b` before opening `a`'s transform — which is
//          `examples/Ledger` FINDING 2's validate-then-apply, arriving from a
//          third direction.
fn case11(a &Synchronized<int>, b &Synchronized<int>) {
	a.update(fn (&var x) {
		x += b.with(fn (y) { return *y })
	})
}

// ── 12. UNFINISHED — a conformance the fields deny ───────────────────────────
// RULE     `aliasing.md`: a type is `Copy` only if every field is.
// WANTED   To make a wrapper cheap to pass around without thinking about what
//          it holds.
// WHY      Field-wise `Copy` is the whole mechanism — no propagation rule, no
//          contagion rule. A declaration that contradicts it is the one way to
//          get silent duplication of a mutable handle, which is the original
//          `Copy` bug this design was reorganised to kill.
// SAYS     — **cannot be written** to the standard the rest of the design
//          holds. "`Mixed` cannot be `Copy`" is easy; the useful message names
//          the field and its type and says why — and `aliasing.md` states
//          eligibility as *necessary rather than sufficient* without saying
//          what a contradicting declaration produces.
// INSTEAD  `Mixed implements Move`, and a hand-written `.alias()` if a second
//          handle is wanted. That is `examples/Buffer`'s shape exactly.
export object Mixed {
	const shared Confined<Doc>,
}

Mixed implements Copy

// ── 13. UNCAUGHT — cancellation unwinding a transform ────────────────────────
// RULE     none.
// WANTED   An ordinary read-modify-write that happens to call something slow.
// WHY      `reactivity.md` makes cancellation cooperative: it lands at the next
//          yield point and unwinds the stack. A transform may call anything, so
//          it may yield. The unwind leaves `lines` with "start" and no "end".
// SAYS     — nothing. There is no failure path on the page, so there is no
//          line to attach a message to, and all three candidate answers to the
//          partial-write defect in `aliasing.md` are about failures the author
//          *wrote*.
// INSTEAD  — nothing. This is the one pair `examples/Dashboard` classed as
//          **unsound** rather than as a gap or a bad program: it cannot be
//          written correctly today.
fn case13(state &Synchronized<Doc>, from int) {
	state.update(fn (&var doc) {
		doc.lines.append("start")
		slowNetworkThing(from)       // yields; cancellation lands here
		doc.lines.append("end")
	})
}

fn slowNetworkThing(n int) {}

// ── 14. UNCAUGHT — a lock taken on the cell thread ───────────────────────────
// RULE     none. Every rule is satisfied.
// WANTED   The authoritative count rather than a sampled copy, which is the
//          more careful instinct and is what makes this dangerous.
// WHY      The UI thread blocks on a worker's lock, inside a turn that
//          `reactivity.md` gives a frame budget. Nothing is unsound; the
//          program is just janky under load, which is the hardest class of bug
//          to attribute.
// SAYS     — nothing today. It should be a **lint** rather than an error,
//          because the same call is correct off the cell thread: something
//          like "`with` takes a lock, and this runs on the cell thread".
//          Naming it needs the thread-affinity body property.
// INSTEAD  Read the `Published<T>` copy, which is what it is for.
fn case14(counts &Synchronized<int>) -> int {
	return counts.with(fn (n) { return *n })
}

// ── 15. UNCAUGHT — a coroutine outliving its component ───────────────────────
// RULE     none.
// WANTED   To start some work from a component without wiring it into an
//          `effect`.
// WHY      `reactivity.md` ends an `effect`'s coroutine by cancelling it when
//          the component goes away. A `background` spawned from an ordinary
//          `fn` has no stated owner, so the work outlives the screen and the
//          type system never notices.
// SAYS     — nothing. The honest message is "`background` here has no owner",
//          which requires the language to have a notion of ownership for
//          coroutines, and `grammar-script-notes.md` says only that the
//          runtime owns them.
// INSTEAD  Spawn it in an `effect`, which `examples/Dashboard` does — and
//          nothing makes that the obvious choice rather than a convention.
fn case15(doc Shared<Doc>) {
	background(fn {
		doc.get().lines.length()
	})
}

// ── 16. UNCAUGHT — a macro that does not terminate ───────────────────────────
// RULE     none. `functions.md` states that `pure` does not imply termination.
// WANTED   A generated declaration per something, with the loop condition
//          wrong — which is an ordinary bug, not a malicious one.
// WHY      The compile-time evaluator runs this. A compiler that hangs on a
//          typo is the failure mode every macro system has shipped at least
//          once.
// SAYS     — nothing yet, and the message is already decided in shape:
//          `macros.md` needs a step budget, so it is "macro `Forever` exceeded
//          N steps". What is missing is N and the plumbing, not the design.
// INSTEAD  — not applicable; the point is the budget, not the program.
@macro
pure fn Forever(node Declaration) -> [Declaration] {
	var out [Declaration] = []

	while true {
		out.append(node)
	}

	return out
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// WHAT WRITING THE MESSAGES FOUND.
//
// The file was written to check whether the rules fire. Adding SAYS to each
// case turned it into something more useful, because **a rule whose message
// cannot be written is a rule that is not finished.**
//
// That is a mechanical test and it sorted the file by itself. All five
// UNFINISHED cases failed it for the same reason: the message would have to
// name something nobody has decided — how long an iterator's borrow lasts
// (case 8), what an assignable index *is* (9), the allocates-or-not property
// (10), the body-position mechanism that lost `pure` (11), and what a
// contradicting conformance produces (12). None of them is a hole in the
// design. All of them are the distance between a decision and a checker, and
// a project with no compiler cannot otherwise tell the two apart.
//
// The four UNCAUGHT cases split differently and the split is worth keeping:
//
//   - case 13 **cannot be written correctly today**. It is unsound and it is
//     the shortest fuse in the record.
//   - cases 14 and 15 are *lints*, not errors — the same call is correct in
//     another context, which is `diagnostics.md`'s register exactly. Both
//     need the thread-affinity body property to have something to name.
//   - case 16 is already designed in shape and missing only a number.
//
// And one REFUSED case is worth revisiting rather than celebrating. Case 6
// forbids a `pure fn` from taking an accumulator, and an accumulator is not
// impure in any interesting sense — `examples/Json`'s writer is exactly this
// shape and simply does not say `pure`. The rule is defensible and it may be
// stricter than it needs to be, which is the kind of thing only a wrong
// program surfaces.
//
// Sixteen ways to be wrong; **nine not currently caught**; one with no correct
// spelling at all.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// vim: ft=kotlin
